// br jefm9w: arg_tids_match_mono_args, the struct-literal seam's tid-vs-CSV parity veto, is
// RETIRED — deleted at all four call sites and as a `fn`. This file used to pin the programs
// that proved it live; it now pins the same programs as anti-regressions for the producer
// fixes that made the veto's only genuine job (site 3, br phybxe) obsolete, plus the false
// positive its removal fixes.
//
// The veto used to compare a recovered arg_tids list against an already-chosen mono_args CSV
// segment by segment and discard the WHOLE list on any positive contradiction. br qnpb2d's
// rule is THE TID IS AUTHORITATIVE — when the tid and the string CSV disagree the tid is
// correct and the string is a stale or aliased spelling — so once nothing upstream hands the
// tier a tid belonging to the WRONG instance, there is nothing left to arbitrate.
//
// ── THE FIRST ATTEMPT, WHY IT REVERTED ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A prior session deleted the veto at all four sites and measured a byte-identical pinned
// corpus (747 files, 732 reaching codegen) with `task regen` and all tests green — then found
// by hand, off-corpus, that it regressed two programs the sweep never touched:
//
//   Box[Set[Int]]  via a field-access receiver     HEAD green -> POST cc ERROR   (row B)
//   Box[List[Int]] via a field-access receiver     HEAD green -> POST cc ERROR   (row B2)
//
// Both are the SAME defect: a struct literal in FIELD-ACCESS RECEIVER position (`Box { value:
// Box { value: Set() } }.value`) was typed as the RESULT of the access rather than as itself,
// so the OUTER literal (really Box[Box[Set[Int]]]) matched the enclosing let's annotation
// (Box[Set[Int]]) by bare struct name and adopted `Set` one nesting level too high. Site 3's
// veto happened to decline that adoption and kept the instances distinct; deleting the veto
// let the outer collapse onto the inner and `cc` failed. That was a producer bug wearing the
// veto as an accidental partial mask, not a reason to keep the veto: it was filed as br
// qah9tx and fixed upstream in 591d4a1 — the receiver-seam gets its own annotation, retargeted
// to itself the same way htxpmh's field-loop retarget already covers field-value position.
//
// ── RE-MEASURED POST-FIX, AT 1b96cec ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// With qah9tx's fix in the binary, row B's shape no longer reaches site 3 at all: the tid
// tier admits Box[Set[Int]] directly (0 `decline=nesting_level_mismatch`, `tid_wins` true), so
// the Class-A recovery this file used to pin as load-bearing never runs. Rows B and B2 are
// anti-regressions for qah9tx now, not veto liveness proofs — they still assert the same C
// shapes, because those shapes are what "typed as itself" looks like, but the mechanism
// answering them moved from the veto to the fixed producer before the veto was ever deleted.
//
// The false positive the veto DID own outright is still live at HEAD and is what its deletion
// actually fixes — see ROW G below.
//
// ── ROW LEDGER ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   ROW A   P[Alias, Box[Int]] into a P[Int, Box[Int]] param   green, control (unchanged)
//   ROW B   Box[Set[Int]] via a field-access receiver          green, qah9tx anti-regression
//   ROW B2  Box[List[Int]] via a field-access receiver         green, qah9tx anti-regression
//   ROW C   Box[Box[Int]]                                      green, htxpmh anti-regression
//   ROW D   Box[Box[Option[Str]]] over a bare None             green, htxpmh anti-regression
//   ROW E   the Some("z") twin                                 green, htxpmh anti-regression
//   ROW F   Box[Alias] — used to trip the guard, ran anyway    green, control
//   ROW G   P[Alias, I64] — the veto's own false positive      HEAD I0001 -> green (jefm9w)
//
// ── ROW G: THE FALSE POSITIVE THE VETO CAUSED, AND WHY DELETING IT IS THE RIGHT FIX ────────
//
// The veto could not tell a wrong nesting level from a second spelling of one type, so it also
// rejected legitimate aliased slots, and its rejection was not a graceful degrade: the
// recovered list was discarded, the binder survived to emission, and the program died on
// I0001 UnsolvedTypeVarAtCodegen. Measured at 1a0f961, while the veto was still live:
//
//   type Alias = Int
//   type P[A, B] { a: A, b: B }
//   let p: P[Alias, I64] = P { a: 1, b: 2 }
//
//   [retmono] sname=P decline=nesting_level_mismatch csv=Alias,Int
//   [site3 classA]  verdict=DIVERGE segs=[Alias,Int] csv=[Alias,I64]
//   internal compiler error[UnsolvedTypeVarAtCodegen]: the def-side slot resolved to 'B'
//
// Two spellings, one type, on both slots: `type Alias = Int` mints its OWN tid, and
// resolve_type_name folds I64 onto TYPE_INT (src/typecheck.bl:1013) so the tid spells "Int"
// where the annotation's bare node name spells "I64". This was never a nesting problem — there
// is no nested literal here at all — so the veto's own contract (catch a tid recovered off a
// MISMATCHED nesting level) never applied; it fired anyway because it cannot distinguish an
// aliased spelling from a genuine contradiction. Canonicalizing alias tids at the seg speller
// (the q38hbk family) would also fix this row, but with the veto gone there is no consumer
// left to canonicalize FOR — deleting the arbitration is the smaller, sufficient fix, and
// q38hbk is not needed to close this row.
//
// Arming the comparator to an ICE instead of deleting it was refused twice, and rows A, B and
// B2 are why: an ICE would have turned three working programs into hard compiler errors for a
// veto whose only genuine catch (site 3) was itself a mask over a producer bug now fixed at
// its source.

import compile_test_helpers
import std.io

fn assert_has(c: Str, needle: Str, label: Str) {
    if !c.contains(needle) { io.println("FAIL: {label} — missing '{needle}'") }
    assert(c.contains(needle))
}

fn assert_lacks(c: Str, needle: Str, label: Str) {
    if c.contains(needle) { io.println("FAIL: {label} — forbidden '{needle}' present") }
    assert(!c.contains(needle))
}

fn box_decl() -> Str {
    "type Box[T] \{ value: T }\n"
}

// ── ROW A: an aliased slot beside a compound slot — CONTROL ───────────────────────────────
//
// `Alias` and `Int` are one type under two spellings, and slot 1 is a COMPOUND, which is the
// combination that puts the two spellings in front of the veto. The instance is named
// blink_P_Int_Box_Int and matches take()'s parameter type exactly.
//
// This row was originally recorded as a second liveness proof, on a reading that the deletion
// renamed it to blink_P_Alias_Box_Int and broke the call at cc. That reading was an ARTEFACT
// of measuring against a partially-rebuilt compiler. Re-measured on a clean worktree with a
// full regen, the emitted C is byte-identical with and without the deletion and the program
// is green both ways. It is kept as a control: it pins the canonicalization that makes the
// call typecheck, and it documents that this shape is NOT what makes the veto load-bearing.
fn src_alias_compound_param() -> Str {
    let mut s = "type Alias = Int\n"
    s = s.concat(box_decl())
    s = s.concat("type P[A, B] \{ a: A, b: B }\n")
    s = s.concat("fn take(p: P[Int, Box[Int]]) -> Int \{ p.a }\n")
    s = s.concat("fn main() \{\n")
    s = s.concat("    let q: P[Alias, Box[Int]] = P \{ a: 1, b: Box \{ value: 2 } }\n")
    s = s.concat("    assert(take(q) == 1)\n")
    s = s.concat("}\n")
    s
}

test "an aliased slot beside a compound slot canonicalizes to the param's spelling" {
    let c = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_emit_c(src_alias_compound_param(), "jefm9w_ra_c")
    assert_has(c, "blink_P_Int_Box_Int", "row A / instance matches the param type")
    assert_lacks(c, "blink_P_Alias_Box_Int", "row A / nominally distinct alias-spelled twin")
    assert_lacks(c, "BLINK_I0001_", "row A / I0001 sentinel")
    let r = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_run(src_alias_compound_param(), "jefm9w_ra_run")
    if r.exit_code != 0 { io.println("FAIL: row A runtime — {r.err_out}") }
    assert(r.exit_code == 0)
}

// ── ROW B: the qah9tx receiver-seam fix, anti-regression ──────────────────────────────────
//
// The literal sits in FIELD-ACCESS RECEIVER position, which htxpmh's field-loop retarget does
// not cover: the enclosing `let` annotates the RESULT of `.value`, not the receiver. Before
// br qah9tx the outer literal — really Box[Box[Set[Int]]] — saw cg_let_target_ann still
// spelling Box[Set[Int]] and, because tid_native_struct_inst_tid matches value-context
// annotations by BARE STRUCT NAME, adopted `Set` one nesting level too high. The veto (site 3's
// Class-A recovery) used to decline that adoption and kept both instances distinct — an
// accidental partial mask, not a fix, since the same defect broke the scalar-payload and
// two-level-chain twins of this shape that the veto could not catch at all (pinned green in
// tests/test_qah9tx_fieldaccess_receiver.bl).
//
// br qah9tx fixed the receiver seam itself: the literal is now typed as ITSELF rather than as
// the access result, so the outer instance is correctly Box[Box[Set[Int]]] from the start and
// the tid tier admits it directly — 0 `decline=nesting_level_mismatch`, site 3 never runs.
// With the veto now deleted entirely, this row pins that the producer fix alone carries it:
//
//   typedef struct { blink_set*    value; } blink_Box_Set;
//   typedef struct { blink_Box_Set value; } blink_Box_Box_Set;
//
// The runtime assertion guards the other half: the Set must keep the int kops vtable rather
// than degrading to blink_kops_str, which is the silent hnyqe4 collision class.
fn src_receiver_nested_set() -> Str {
    let mut s = box_decl()
    s = s.concat("fn main() \{\n")
    s = s.concat("    let mut n: Box[Set[Int]] = Box \{ value: Box \{ value: Set() } }.value\n")
    s = s.concat("    n.value.insert(256)\n")
    s = s.concat("    n.value.insert(512)\n")
    s = s.concat("    assert(n.value.contains(256))\n")
    s = s.concat("    assert(n.value.contains(512))\n")
    s = s.concat("    assert(n.value.contains(7) == false)\n")
    s = s.concat("}\n")
    s
}

test "a struct literal in field-access-receiver position keeps its own nesting level" {
    let c = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_emit_c(src_receiver_nested_set(), "jefm9w_rb_c")
    assert_has(c, "blink_Box_Box_Set", "row B / outer instance exists")
    assert_has(c, "blink_Box_Set value;", "row B / outer field lowers to the inner mono")
    assert_lacks(c, "blink_set* value;\n} blink_Box_Box_Set", "row B / outer collapsed onto inner")
    assert_has(c, "blink_kops_i64", "row B / int-keyed Set gets the int vtable")
    assert_lacks(c, "blink_kops_str", "row B / int keys hashed through the string vtable")
    assert_lacks(c, "BLINK_I0001_", "row B / I0001 sentinel")
    let r = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_run(src_receiver_nested_set(), "jefm9w_rb_run")
    if r.exit_code != 0 { io.println("FAIL: row B runtime — {r.err_out}") }
    assert(r.exit_code == 0)
}

// ── ROW B2: the same seam with a different carrier ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// List instead of Set, to pin that row B is about the RECEIVER POSITION and not about Set's
// kops resolution. Same qah9tx fix, same producer, same anti-regression role.
fn src_receiver_nested_list() -> Str {
    let mut s = box_decl()
    s = s.concat("fn main() \{\n")
    s = s.concat("    let mut n: Box[List[Int]] = Box \{ value: Box \{ value: [] } }.value\n")
    s = s.concat("    n.value.push(7)\n")
    s = s.concat("    assert(n.value.len() == 1)\n")
    s = s.concat("}\n")
    s
}

test "a List-carrying literal in field-access-receiver position keeps its own nesting level" {
    let c = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_emit_c(src_receiver_nested_list(), "jefm9w_rb2_c")
    assert_has(c, "blink_Box_Box_List", "row B2 / outer instance exists")
    assert_has(c, "blink_Box_List value;", "row B2 / outer field lowers to the inner mono")
    assert_lacks(c, "BLINK_I0001_", "row B2 / I0001 sentinel")
    let r = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_run(src_receiver_nested_list(), "jefm9w_rb2_run")
    if r.exit_code != 0 { io.println("FAIL: row B2 runtime — {r.err_out}") }
    assert(r.exit_code == 0)
}

// ── ROW C: the nested-adoption anti-regression ────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The plain form of row B's shape, in a position htxpmh's retarget DOES cover. Without the
// retarget the inner literal would also see Box[Box[Int]] and name itself blink_Box_Box_Int,
// lowering its own `value` field to blink_Box_Int — self-referential and undeclared. The same
// needles live in tests/test_qnpb2d_tid_outranks_target.bl, where they prove the retarget;
// here they pin that the retarget, not the veto, is what carries this row — which is why row B
// is a producer gap and not a second copy of this one.
fn src_box_box_int() -> Str {
    let mut s = box_decl()
    s = s.concat("fn main() \{\n")
    s = s.concat("    let b: Box[Box[Int]] = Box \{ value: Box \{ value: 7 } }\n")
    s = s.concat("    assert(b.value.value == 7)\n")
    s = s.concat("}\n")
    s
}

test "a nested struct-instance literal in field-value position is correctly named" {
    let c = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_emit_c(src_box_box_int(), "jefm9w_rc_c")
    assert_has(c, "blink_Box_Box_Int", "row C / outer symbol")
    assert_has(c, "blink_Box_Int value;", "row C / field lowers to the inner mono")
    assert_lacks(c, "blink_Box_Box_Int value;", "row C / self-referential typedef")
    assert_lacks(c, "BLINK_I0001_", "row C / I0001 sentinel")
    let r = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_run(src_box_box_int(), "jefm9w_rc_run")
    if r.exit_code != 0 { io.println("FAIL: row C runtime — {r.err_out}") }
    assert(r.exit_code == 0)
}

// ── ROWS D/E: the shapes the guard cannot catch ───────────────────────────────────────────
//
// htxpmh's headline reds, kept here as the other half of row C's argument. The guard treats a
// -1 slot as a DON'T-CARE rather than a contradiction, so a bare `None` carrier — whose slot
// reverse-infers unencodable — slips straight past it. These rows are carried ENTIRELY by the
// retarget, so they bound what the veto is responsible for: it is not a general backstop for
// nesting adoption, only for the sites the retarget misses.
fn src_nested_opt(payload: Str) -> Str {
    let mut s = box_decl()
    s = s.concat("fn main() \{\n")
    s = s.concat("    let x: Box[Box[Option[Str]]] = Box \{ value: Box \{ value: {payload} } }\n")
    s = s.concat("    assert(true)\n")
    s = s.concat("}\n")
    s
}

test "a nested bare-None carrier stays at its own nesting level" {
    let c = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_emit_c(src_nested_opt("None"), "jefm9w_rd_c")
    assert_lacks(c, "BLINK_I0001_", "row D / I0001 sentinel")
    assert_lacks(c, "Box_Box_Box_Option_str", "row D / mono name one nesting level too deep")
    assert_has(c, "blink_Box_Box_Option_str", "row D / outer instance")
    assert_has(c, "blink_Box_Option_str", "row D / inner instance kept distinct")
    let r = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_run(src_nested_opt("None"), "jefm9w_rd_run")
    if r.exit_code != 0 { io.println("FAIL: row D runtime — {r.err_out}") }
    assert(r.exit_code == 0)
}

test "a nested Some carrier stays at its own nesting level" {
    let c = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_emit_c(src_nested_opt("Some(\"z\")"), "jefm9w_re_c")
    assert_lacks(c, "BLINK_I0001_", "row E / I0001 sentinel")
    assert_lacks(c, "erased_slot", "row E / erased mono slot")
    assert_has(c, "blink_Box_Box_Option_str", "row E / outer instance")
    assert_has(c, "blink_Box_Option_str", "row E / inner instance kept distinct")
    let r = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_run(src_nested_opt("Some(\"z\")"), "jefm9w_re_run")
    if r.exit_code != 0 { io.println("FAIL: row E runtime — {r.err_out}") }
    assert(r.exit_code == 0)
}

// ── ROW F: the control that separates row A's two mechanisms ─────────────────────────────
//
// `Box[Alias]` used to trip the site-1 guard for the same reason row G does — the
// annotation's slot spells "Alias" while the literal's own reverse-inferred slot spells
// "Int" — but with one scalar slot and no compound beside it there was nothing left to
// diverge downstream, so the guard's decline handed the literal to a string tier that named
// it identically and the program stayed green either way. Measured at 1a0f961, while the
// guard was still live:
//
//   [retmono] sname=Box decline=nesting_level_mismatch csv=Alias
//   [site3 classA]  verdict=match segs=[Alias] csv=[Alias]
//
// So a nesting_level_mismatch decline was NEVER on its own evidence of a bug — it fired here
// exactly as often as on row G below, and only the presence of a SECOND slot (row G's `B`)
// turned the same aliased spelling into an I0001. With the guard deleted this row is admitted
// directly by the tid tier with no arbitration at all; it stays as the one-scalar-slot control
// against row G's two-slot break.
fn src_box_alias() -> Str {
    let mut s = "type Alias = Int\n"
    s = s.concat(box_decl())
    s = s.concat("fn main() \{\n")
    s = s.concat("    let b: Box[Alias] = Box \{ value: 7 }\n")
    s = s.concat("    assert(b.value == 7)\n")
    s = s.concat("}\n")
    s
}

test "a lone aliased slot compiles and runs" {
    let c = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_emit_c(src_box_alias(), "jefm9w_rf_c")
    assert_lacks(c, "BLINK_I0001_", "row F / I0001 sentinel")
    assert_lacks(c, "_Void", "row F / erasure")
    let r = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_run(src_box_alias(), "jefm9w_rf_run")
    if r.exit_code != 0 { io.println("FAIL: row F runtime — {r.err_out}") }
    assert(r.exit_code == 0)
}

// ── ROW G: the veto's own false positive, now fixed ───────────────────────────────────────
//
// `P[Alias, I64]` puts an aliased slot BESIDE a second slot, unlike row F's lone alias. Both
// slots disagree with the annotation's spelling — `Alias`/`Int` and `I64`/`Int` — which used
// to be exactly two positive contradictions for the veto to catch, and it discarded the whole
// recovered list rather than degrading one slot at a time. With no slot recovered, the def-side
// substitution never landed and the program died at the I0001 chokepoint (measured at 1a0f961,
// while the veto was still live):
//
//   [retmono] sname=P decline=nesting_level_mismatch csv=Alias,Int
//   [site3 classA]  verdict=DIVERGE segs=[Alias,Int] csv=[Alias,I64]
//   internal compiler error[UnsolvedTypeVarAtCodegen]: the def-side slot resolved to 'B'
//
// This was never a nesting-level bug — there is no nested literal in this program at all — so
// the veto's contract never covered it; it fired only because it could not tell an aliased
// spelling from a genuine contradiction. Deleting the veto is the fix: the tid, not the string
// CSV, is what the def side lowers from, and br qnpb2d already settled that the tid is
// authoritative. This is the row the deletion is FOR.
fn src_alias_two_slot() -> Str {
    let mut s = "type Alias = Int\n"
    s = s.concat("type P[A, B] \{ a: A, b: B }\n")
    s = s.concat("fn main() \{\n")
    s = s.concat("    let p: P[Alias, I64] = P \{ a: 1, b: 2 }\n")
    s = s.concat("    assert(p.a == 1)\n")
    s = s.concat("    assert(p.b == 2)\n")
    s = s.concat("}\n")
    s
}

test "an aliased slot beside a second slot no longer dies at I0001" {
    let c = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_emit_c(src_alias_two_slot(), "jefm9w_rg_c")
    assert_lacks(c, "BLINK_I0001_", "row G / I0001 sentinel")
    let r = compile_test_helpers.compile_and_run(src_alias_two_slot(), "jefm9w_rg_run")
    if r.exit_code != 0 { io.println("FAIL: row G runtime — {r.err_out}") }
    assert(r.exit_code == 0)
}
